lesson: 161
title: Multiplying bigger numbers in stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When there are many equal groups, multiply the number of groups by the size of one group, even when both are two-digit numbers.
explain: Split one number into tens and ones: 23 boxes of 34 pens is 20 × 34 + 3 × 34 = 680 + 102 = 782 pens.
explain: It does not matter which number you write first: 34 × 23 is the same 782.
vars: a=name p=12..48 b=11..35 x=2..9
need: p * b <= 1500
answer: p * b
exercise: A shop gets {b} boxes of pencils. Each box holds {p} pencils. How many pencils does the shop get?
exercise: There are {b} rows of seats in a hall and {p} seats in every row. How many seats are there in the hall?
exercise: {a} reads {p} pages every day for {b} days. How many pages does {a.he} read in all?
exercise: Farmer {a} plants {b} rows of trees with {p} trees in each row. How many trees does {a.he} plant?
exercise: One bus carries {p} children. How many children can {b} such buses carry?
exercise: A school buys {b} packs of paper. Each pack has {p} sheets. How many sheets is that?
exercise: Each crate holds {p} bottles of juice. How many bottles are there in {b} crates?
test: How many eggs are there altogether if a farm sends out {b} trays and every tray holds {p} eggs?
test: A worker makes {p} bricks an hour. How many bricks does the worker make in {b} hours?
test: {a}'s class collected {b} bags of cans, {p} cans in a bag, and carried them in {x} trips. How many cans did they collect?
test: Find the number of chairs in a theatre with {b} rows if every row has {p} chairs.
test: A train has {b} carriages and {p} people sit in each carriage. How many people are on the train?
exercise: A school in Texas has {b} classrooms with {p} desks in each. How many desks are there altogether?
exercise: How many crayons are there in {b} boxes if every box holds {p} crayons?
exercise: {a} packs {p} oranges into each crate and fills {b} crates. How many oranges does {a.he} pack in total?
exercise: There are {p} stamps on every page of an album with {b.words} pages. How many stamps are in the album?
exercise: A baker in Manchester bakes {b} trays of rolls, {p} rolls per tray. How many rolls is that in all?
exercise: {b} teams take part in a race in Auckland, and each team has {p.words} runners. How many runners are there combined?
exercise: A stadium in Ohio has {b} sections, and {p} people sit in every section. How many people are in the stadium when it is full?
exercise: Each of {b} pupils brings {p} cents for a class trip. How many cents do they bring together?
test: {p} flowers are planted in each of {b} flower beds in a park in Dublin. Find the total number of flowers.
test: What is the total number of apples on {b} trees in an orchard in Washington State if every tree gives {p} apples?
test: A ferry in Sydney makes {b} trips a day and carries {p} passengers on each trip. How many passengers does it carry in one day?
test: {a} sells {b} boxes of cookies at a fair in Vermont. There are {p} cookies in every box, and {a.he} sells them in {x} hours. How many cookies does {a.he} sell?
test: A car park in Leeds has {b} rows of spaces with {p} spaces in each row. How many cars can park there?
test: A hotel in Toronto has {b} floors, and there are {p} rooms on every floor. How many rooms does the hotel have?
exercise: {b} boxes, {p} pencils each, how many pencils we got
exercise: {b} rows, {p+1} seats a row — no sorry, {p} a row. how many seats
exercise: Sam says {b} buses of {p} kids is {p+b}. nah, how many kids really
exercise: {a} reads {p} pages a day for {b} days, {x} of them days was raining. how many pages {a.he} read
test: {b} crates, {p} bottles a crate, how many bottles is that then
test: {a} reckons {b} trays of {p} eggs is {p*b+10}?? that's wrong innit, how many eggs actually
test: worker does {p} bricks an hour, did {b+1} hours, sorry, {b} hours. how many bricks he made
exercise: Grandad passed away and left {a} {b} boxes of old stamps, with {p} stamps in each box. How many stamps does {a} get?
exercise: uncle got let go from the factory so now he's stuck packing {b} bags of {p} screws a day for some bloke down the road. how many screws a day, for god's sake
test: Dad was economical with the truth: he said he'd only bought a couple of crates. In fact he bought {b} crates with {p} bottles in each. How many bottles did he really buy?
exam: It is given that a shop receives {b} boxes of pencils, each box containing {p} pencils. Determine the aggregate number of pencils received.
exam: Whereas a hall comprises {b} rows of seats, with {p} seats in every row, ascertain the total seating capacity of the hall.
exam: Ascertain the product of the numbers {p} and {b}.
exam: A consignment of {b} crates is dispatched to a market, each crate containing {p} oranges. State the total number of oranges comprised in the consignment.
